@ common block definitions for filenames

@a
      package_init(rf)
      define_var_pk(rf,filenames_max,INT) 
      define_dimen_pk(rf,filenames_size,FILELEN)
      define_dimen_pk(rf,filenames_ind,filenames_max)
      define_varp_pk(rf,filenames_array,CHAR,filenames_size,filenames_ind)

@ declarations for filenames routine

@f rf_common integer 
@m rf_common
	rf_common_a @;


@ indices for the array of filenames
@m elementsfile 1
@m backgroundfile 2
@m testfile 3
@m geometryfile 4
@m problemfile 5
@m reactionfile 6
@m speciesfile 7
@m aladinfile 8
@m aladoutfile 9

@* file names for degas2
each file used by degas2 as input or output has a name which can be specified 
by the user in a file. The filenames are read as variables called
|sybolic_fnames|. These names are easily associated with the contents of
the file.  Each of these goes into an array called
|filenames_array|. To refer to a particular filename, a pointer to this
array is used. The pointers have names that are easy to remember and are
same as the symbolic filenames. They are defined through macros above. A
common block that contains |filenames_array|  is passed to all routines
that will  require a filename. To refer to a file name which has the
sybolic name \em{elementsfile}, we have to refer to
\em{filenames_array[elementsfile]} and to refer to a file with the sybolic
name \em{geometryfile} we refer to \em{filenames_array[geometryfile]} and
so on. The array of filenames can be filled by calling the routine
readfilenames. In summary, the main input file for degas2 contains
something like - 

\begin{verbatim}
elementsfile elements.nc
backgroundfile back.nc
...
...
\end{verbatim}

and we open these files by doing :

rf_common
...

open (unit,file=filenames_array[elementsfile],status='unknown')
open (unit,file=filenames_array[backgroundfile],status='old')
...
...








